= Defeat in Cuba

The war is over.

The American expeditionary force has withdrawn from Cuba after failing to force Spain to surrender. Months of fighting, enormous expenditures, and the loss of thousands of American soldiers have ended without achieving the objectives proclaimed by Congress only months before.

The fleet returns to American ports carrying exhausted veterans instead of triumphant heroes. Across the country, church bells remain silent, victory parades are cancelled, and newspaper headlines that once promised a short and glorious campaign now demand to know who is responsible for the disaster.

Spain celebrates the defense of its Caribbean empire as a remarkable national triumph. Though weakened by civil unrest at home, Madrid has preserved its authority over Cuba and emerged with its honor restored. European observers, many of whom doubted Spain could withstand American intervention, now speak of the conflict as a costly lesson in the limits of American power.

The political consequences are immediate. Republican leaders who championed the war find themselves under relentless attack in Congress and throughout the press. Democrats accuse the administration of wasting American lives in an unnecessary foreign adventure, while even many Republicans privately question whether the nation was truly prepared for an overseas war.

The defeat leaves a lasting scar upon the American military. Calls for sweeping reforms echo through the War and Navy Departments as officers study every mistake made during the campaign. Whether the nation chooses to retreat from foreign affairs or prepare for another opportunity remains to be seen.

- @peace_terms: Negotiate Peace

@peace_terms

Spain has agreed to end hostilities, but victory has given Madrid the stronger position at the negotiating table.

American diplomats must now decide how much they are willing to concede in order to secure the return of prisoners, end the conflict, and restore normal relations.

- @pay_indemnity: Pay an indemnity to Spain.
- @white_peace: Negotiate a white peace.

@pay_indemnity
subtitle: End the war at any cost.
