    Q.dem_bourbon_strength += 5;
    Q.radical_dissent += 2;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 2;
!}

After weeks of negotiation, both governments agree to restore the status quo.

Spain retains Cuba and Puerto Rico, while the United States abandons all claims arising from the conflict. Although no indemnity is paid, many Americans still view the treaty as a humiliating failure after so much blood and treasure were spent for no tangible gain.

The Republican administration survives, but its prestige has been severely damaged.

- @End: Continue

@End

The nation now turns inward.

Questions that seemed settled only months before dominate political debate once again. Should the United States abandon dreams of overseas intervention? Can the military be trusted to defend American interests abroad? And above all, who should bear responsibility for the defeat?

The next election will answer those questions.